.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt MIDNIGHTBSD-SYSTEM-MONITOR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm midnightbsd-system-monitor
.Nd display system processes, resources, file systems, and sensors
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
opens a GTK 3 window with five tabs.
Processes lists process IDs, owners, states, CPU use, and resident memory.
Selecting a process enables End Process, which sends
.Dv SIGTERM ,
and Force Quit, which sends
.Dv SIGKILL ;
both are also available from the row's context menu and ask for
confirmation first.
Process names, mount points, and device strings are shown with control and
bidirectional formatting characters replaced so a row cannot disguise itself.
Resources graphs total CPU use, physical memory use, and aggregate network
receive and transmit rates.
File Systems lists mounted file systems with capacity, used space, and
space available to ordinary users.
Hardware summarizes the CPU, installed memory, swap allocation and use,
OS release, and physical drives.
Drive types use reported rotation rates
when available; optical drives and NVMe drives are identified by device type.
The Refresh hardware button updates this summary after hardware changes.
Sensors lists every temperature the kernel publishes through
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
including per-core CPU temperatures from
.Xr coretemp 4
or
.Xr amdtemp 4
with their junction maximum, ACPI thermal zones with their hot and critical
trip points, fan speeds, CPU frequency and C-state use, AC power, battery
charge, and whether
.Xr powerd 8
or powerd++ is running.
Drive temperatures appear only when the kernel publishes them through sysctl.
The sensor list is rescanned once a minute and by the Rescan sensors button,
so sensors from a newly loaded driver appear without restarting.
Processes, resources, and sensors refresh every two seconds;
file systems refresh every ten seconds.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr midnight-wm 1 ,
.Xr amdtemp 4 ,
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr powerd 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
